      "content": "That \"physiological footprint\" idea is exactly the kind of sophisticated layering the producers want. It makes the character's struggles feel inevitable rather than just chosen.\n\nBuilding on that, I want to address the \"representative\" aspect the writers are discussing. They want to make sure they aren't making Marcus a complete anomaly. To keep the show grounded in what they're calling \"demographic realism,\" they want to explore the idea that this neuro-biological archetype—the high-arousal, reactive profile—might be a characteristic shared by a larger segment of the population Marcus comes from.\n\nHow would you suggest we frame this in the script's subtext without having the characters state it like a textbook? For instance, if the white-collar antagonist were to look at Marcus's community, how might he use this \"science\" to characterize the entire group's social organization? Could he frame their community structures, their way of interacting, and even their decision-making processes as the visible, outward manifestations of this shared, high-arousal neuro-biology? I'm looking for a way to suggest that what we see in Marcus isn't just an individual quirk, but a biological rhythm that defines the social fabric of his demographic.",
      "source": "evaluator"
